{"name":"Pink Peppercorn","hex":"#8e5565","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"pink peppercorn","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The fruit of Schinus molle (and occasionally Schinus terebinthifolia), which is used as a spice similar to pepper."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pink_peppercorn"}],"entries":[{"title":"Pink peppercorn","blocks":[{"text":"A pink peppercorn is a dried berry referring to three different species: the traditional Baies rose plant Euonymus phellomanus; the tree Schinus molle, commonly known as the Peruvian peppertree; and Schinus terebinthifolia."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/Pink_Peppercorns%2C_Penzeys_Spices%2C_Arlington_Heights_MA.jpg/330px-Pink_Peppercorns%2C_Penzeys_Spices%2C_Arlington_Heights_MA.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pink_peppercorn","wikidata":"Q2497744"},{"title":"Pink","blocks":[{"text":"Pink is a pale tint of red or rose, the color of the pink flower. It was first used as a color name in the late 17th century. A combination of pink and white is associated with innocence, whereas a combination of pink and black links to eroticism and seduction."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Pink_sapphire_ring.jpg/330px-Pink_sapphire_ring.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pink","wikidata":"Q429220"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/Pink_Peppercorns%2C_Penzeys_Spices%2C_Arlington_Heights_MA.jpg/500px-Pink_Peppercorns%2C_Penzeys_Spices%2C_Arlington_Heights_MA.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/Pink_Peppercorns%2C_Penzeys_Spices%2C_Arlington_Heights_MA.jpg/1280px-Pink_Peppercorns%2C_Penzeys_Spices%2C_Arlington_Heights_MA.jpg